{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And I will harden Pharaoh's heart</b> — They said in Midrash Rabbah (Exodus Rabbah 5:6): He revealed to him that He is destined to harden his heart, in order to execute judgment upon him, because he had enslaved them in harsh labor. And there further (13:4), <b>\"For I have hardened his heart\"</b> (Exodus 10:1) — Rabbi Yochanan said: From here is an opening for the sectarians to say, it was not within his power to repent. Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish said: Let the mouth of the sectarians be stopped! Rather, <b>\"If to the scorners He will scorn\"</b> (Proverbs 3:34) — He warns him a first time and a second and a third, and he does not turn back, so He closes the door of repentance upon him in order to punish him for what he sinned. So too Pharaoh the wicked: since HaShem sent to him five times and he paid no heed to His words, HaShem said to him: You have stiffened your neck and hardened your heart — I will add impurity upon your impurity.\n\nNow, they have explained the question that everyone asks — if HaShem hardened his heart, what is his sin? — and there are two reasons for it, and both are true. The first: because Pharaoh, in his wickedness, perpetrated great evils against Israel for nothing, he deserved to have the ways of repentance withheld from him, as many verses in the Torah and the Writings speak of this, and he is judged according to his earlier deeds. And the second reason: that half of the plagues were upon him through his own fault, for concerning them it is stated only <b>\"and Pharaoh's heart was hardened\"</b> (below verses 13, 22; 8:15) and <b>\"Pharaoh hardened his heart\"</b> (below 8:28; 9:7). He did not wish to send them out for the honor of HaShem; but when the plagues overwhelmed him and he grew weary of bearing them, his heart softened and he considered sending them out because of the severity of the plagues — not to do the will of his Creator. Then HaShem hardened his spirit and strengthened his heart, in order to make His name known, as it is written: <b>\"Thus I will be magnified and sanctified, and I will make Myself known in the eyes of many nations\"</b> etc. (Ezekiel 38:23).\n\nAnd what He said before the plagues (Exodus 4:21), <b>\"And I will harden his heart so that he will not send the people\"</b> — He informs Moses of what will be done to him in the later plagues, as when He said (Exodus 3:19), <b>\"And I know that the king of Egypt will not let you go\"</b>. And this is the meaning of <b>\"And I will harden Pharaoh's heart, and I will multiply My signs\"</b> — that is to say, I will harden his heart in order to multiply My wonders in the land of Egypt. For in the five latter plagues and also at the drowning in the sea it is stated <b>\"and HaShem hardened\"</b> (Exodus 14:8), for <b>\"The king's heart is in the hand of HaShem; wherever He desires He inclines it\"</b> (Proverbs 21:1)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:04:47.084443352Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}